egma-planning/docs/adr/0008-mock-tools-are-served-over-in-room-rpc.md:5:Amended: 2026-08-10, twice, when the decision was built (`mock-tools/spec.md`, implemented). Both are marked in place below. (1) "No egma participant" turns out not to be the whole of what makes the SDK inert — a livekit connection in its token-endpoint shape puts egma in the room and still never reaches the agent. (2) The tool facts observed at the seam grew a third shape, for the call egma turns away. **The decision itself is unchanged in both cases.**
egma-planning/docs/adr/0008-mock-tools-are-served-over-in-room-rpc.md:23:> **Amended 2026-08-10.** What the SDK actually reads is the identity in dispatch metadata, and one shape of a livekit connection never writes any: on the token-endpoint mode the customer's endpoint dispatches their agent, not egma, so egma is in the room and the agent is never told so. The safety property is untouched — absence still means inert, which is what production needs — but the sentence "no egma participant" describes only one of the two ways a room can be silent, and the other one is a simulation that quietly isolated nothing. Recorded in `mock-tools/issues/04-the-simulator-serves-the-seam.md` with the follow-up it deserves.
egma-planning/.scratch/mock-tools/issues/04-the-simulator-serves-the-seam.md:28:- **The token-endpoint shape of a livekit connection can never serve mock tools.** egma does not dispatch on that shape — dispatching is the customer's endpoint's job — so no dispatch metadata is written, egma's participant identity never reaches the agent, and the SDK stays inert inside a real simulation. **Follow-up candidate, not filed, and the sharper half of it:** egma still registers its handlers on that path, so such a simulation lands a stamp of three empty lists — *the asking happened and nothing came back* — where the truth is that nothing could ever have asked. Either the stamp is withheld where no identity was published, or the docs say plainly that mock tools need the key-pair shape.
egma-planning/.scratch/first-simulation-path/issues/04-voice-agent-connection-per-framework.md:36:- **A LiveKit room** covers LiveKit Agents natively, **Retell** (its client SDK is a `livekit-client` wrapper and its `access_token` is a LiveKit JWT), Pipecat when the customer uses `LiveKitTransport`, and **PSTN** by bridging the room with `CreateSIPParticipant`.
egma-planning/.scratch/mock-tools/research/livekit-interception-surface.md:20:- Explicit dispatch carries a `metadata` string both via the Dispatch API and via the joining token's `RoomConfiguration(agents=[RoomAgentDispatch(...)])`. It arrives as `ctx.job.metadata` in the worker's entrypoint. egma's livekit plug already sends its context there — simulation id, modality — per the livekit-connection spec, which deliberately keeps test *content* out. The SDK reads this to know it is in a simulation and which participant identity is egma's. (agents/server/agent-dispatch/, agents/worker/job/)
egma-planning/.scratch/simulation-dispatch/spec.md:70:- The span writer dedups with ClickHouse `insert_deduplication_token` keyed on the writer-minted span ids — explicit id-based dedup replacing reliance on content-hash block identity. Trace identity is derived deterministically from the simulation id, with `egma.simulation_id` carried as an attribute; the ingest learns the simulator's span vocabulary as a scope-gated registry entry beside LiveKit's.
egma-planning/.scratch/voice-transports/research/connection-methods-and-media-backends.md:49:**Raw platform rooms (LiveKit, Daily).** egma joins a room as an ordinary participant, publishes a synthesized audio track, subscribes to the agent's. Both directions of the WebRTC connection are client-initiated (signaling over outbound WSS/HTTPS; media over UDP hole punching with TURN fallback). **Zero inbound surface, by construction.** Pipecat covers both natively: `LiveKitTransport(url, token, room_name, params)` and `DailyTransport(room_url, token, …)`.
egma-planning/.scratch/voice-transports/research/connection-methods-and-media-backends.md:60:The asymmetry matters: Vapi returns the room URL from the API. Retell's `create-web-call` returns **only a bare LiveKit JWT with no room URL** — the host is a constant in a JS bundle that Retell has never acknowledged in writing and can re-point in a patch release with no API-level deprecation signal. A useful Pipecat detail for the future Retell plug: `LiveKitTransport`'s `room_name` argument is **used only in log messages** — the actual connect is `await self.room.connect(self._url, token)`; the real room is encoded in the JWT, so a placeholder string works.
