2026-08-21T06:25:16.529Z	2092	exec	const patch = "*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: /Users/nischalj10/.codex/worktrees/9540/egma/egma-planning/docs/adr/0010-one-versioned-platform-api.md\\n+# ADR-0010: Egma has one versioned platform API\\n+\\n+Date: 2026-08-20\\n+Status: Accepted\\n+\\n+Egma's web app, CLI, and outside clients all act on the same organizations, projects, agents, connections, tests, runs, simulations, personas, graders, and verdicts. They use one customer-facing platform API under `/v1`, rather than separate first-party `/api/*` routes and public routes. One executable definition of each operation owns its method, path, request, response, refusals, and authentication choices; it drives server validation and serialization, the OpenAPI document, and one generated TypeScript client.\\n+\\n+The platform API covers customer-managed Egma resources, including members, invitation management, API keys, monitoring sources, monitoring import failures, trace reads, and recording access. Identity flows, deployment-wide settings, simulator work traffic, OTLP ingestion, and health checks remain separate protocols. Platform resource paths are organization-wide: a project is a write field and an optional read filter, never a path parent. Only a real lifecycle child is nested, such as a connection under its agent, a simulation under its run, or a version under the thing it preserves.\\n+\\n+Resources keep Egma's prefixed, time-sortable identifiers, such as `agt_...`, `tst_...`, and `run_...`, as their one API identifier. Egma does not add a second Google-style relative resource name. Every platform request, response, and query field uses `lowerCamelCase`, such as `projectId`, `createdAt`, `pageToken`, and `idempotencyKey`; generated clients expose the same field names rather than translating casing per language. This renames existing wire fields where necessary but does not change their behavior or scope.\\n+\\n+A growing collection is paginated from its first `/v1` release. Each List response names its resource array and carries `nextPageToken`; the request takes optional `pageSize` and an opaque `pageToken` bound to the other query arguments. Missing or zero size uses the default of 50, a negative size is an invalid request, and a size above the maximum of 200 is clamped. An invalid token, or one used with different query arguments, is an invalid request. Platform refusals use one `{ error, message, details? }` shape: code reads `error`, a person reads the actionable `message`, and structured recovery facts live in a code-specific `details` schema rather than in free-form JSON or text a client must parse.\\n+\\n+The web app and CLI use that generated client directly. They keep only work that belongs to their task: page state and navigation in the web app; platform selection, the egma folder, `pull`, `push`, run following, and terminal output in the CLI. They do not restate paths, payload types, response decoders, or one wrapper per operation. The server remains the authority for request validation and platform preconditions.\\n+\\n+`CreateAgent` creates only an agent, and `CreateConnection` creates one connection under an agent. There is no platform operation that atomically registers both, reuses a provider agent, or adds a connection as a side effect of agent creation. A new agent can therefore exist without a connection. A web or CLI workflow can call both operations in sequence; if connection creation fails, it keeps the new agent and retries only the connection.\\n+\\n+The public lifecycle action is Delete. A delete hides the resource from normal lists and new work, while historical records that refer to it remain readable. The storage layer may implement this as archiving, but the platform API does not expose separate archive and restore actions. Stable server-owned catalogs, such as connection options, capability definitions, persona model options, and grader library entries, are platform resources; a screen's form layout is not.\\n+\\n+The platform API is explicit rather than inferred from every Fastify route or every path beginning with `/v1`. Better Auth and Egma's current account and device-login routes stay outside its OpenAPI document. Egma does not add a separate versioned auth contract until a real public account-lifecycle client needs one. Private simulator claim, heartbeat, and report traffic also remains separate. `POST /v1/traces` remains OTLP and is excluded even though platform trace reads use `GET` at the same path; `/health` remains unversioned.\\n+\\n+The existing TypeScript-to-Python simulation JSON Schemas and private worker routes do not change. A phone run checks for a complete carrier route inside run start or simulation rerun and returns a documented refusal when it is missing; clients do not read a public phone-readiness status first. The current self-host settings door remains a narrow protocol: on a single-organization deployment, an organization administrator uses it to set the carrier route. Egma does not add a new operator identity or operator API. A run becomes complete only after execution and grading finish; execution completion remains a separate progress fact.\\n+\\n+The current platform resource routes move together from `/api/*` to `/v1/*`, and their in-repository callers move in the same change. There are no compatibility aliases. Additive changes remain in `/v1`; a breaking platform-API change requires `/v2`. OpenAPI generation includes only the explicit platform operations, so private worker messages and credentials cannot enter the public document by path accident.\\n+\\n+This supersedes [ADR-0009](0009-the-cli-and-the-platform-ship-together.md). The versioned platform API replaces `repository_contract` and the separate internal CLI contract.\\n+\\n+## Considered alternatives\\n+\\n+- **Keep one internal first-party contract and add a second public contract.** Rejected because both contracts would model the same platform resources and would drift.\\n+- **Provide an atomic agent-registration operation.** Rejected because agent and connection are separate resources. Atomicity would also keep provider-specific reuse policy inside the platform API.\\n+- **Maintain OpenAPI separately from executable route schemas.** Rejected because paths and payload shapes would have two authorities.\\n+- **Generate OpenAPI from every registered route.** Rejected because simulator worker, authentication, OTLP, and operations routes are separate protocols and some carry private data.\\n*** Delete File: /Users/nischalj10/.codex/worktrees/9540/egma/egma-planning/docs/adr/0010-one-versioned-product-http-contract.md\\n*** Update File: /Users/nischalj10/.codex/worktrees/9540/egma/egma-planning/docs/index.md\\n@@\\n-  - [0010](adr/0010-one-versioned-product-http-contract.md) — one `/v1` product contract drives validation, OpenAPI, and the shared generated client\\n+  - [0010](adr/0010-one-versioned-platform-api.md) — one `/v1` platform API drives validation, OpenAPI, and the shared generated client\\n*** Update File: /Users/nischalj10/.codex/worktrees/9540/egma/egma-planning/docs/adr/0011-agent-repositories-bind-to-a-platform-url.md\\n@@\\n-Platform selection remains: explicit `--url`, then `EGMA_URL`, then the repository binding, then hosted egma for an unbound repository. The selected operation reports authentication, availability, and missing-resource failures. `egma self-host up` uses a health check for startup. There is no public platform identity or discovery response, no server-origin comparison, and no `repository_contract`; the versioned product HTTP contract owns compatibility.\\n+Platform selection remains: explicit `--url`, then `EGMA_URL`, then the repository binding, then hosted egma for an unbound repository. The selected operation reports authentication, availability, and missing-resource failures. `egma self-host up` uses a health check for startup. There is no public platform identity or discovery response, no server-origin comparison, and no `repository_contract`; the versioned platform API owns compatibility.\\n*** Update File: /Users/nischalj10/.codex/worktrees/9540/egma/egma-planning/CONTEXT.md\\n@@\\n **simulation** — one test executed once, inside a run. One conversation, start to finish. Anthropic's *trial*.\\n@@\\n **simulator** — the component that executes a simulation: it brings the persona to life, holds the conversation with the agent over a connection, and emits the transcript, outcome, metrics and spans. It runs inside egma's own deployment, hosted or self-hosted — never anywhere else. Earlier records call it *the runtime* or *the simulation runtime*; `simulator` is the canonical name, adopted 2026-08-03 when the simulator effort began.\\n+\\n+## Production monitoring\\n+\\n+**monitoring source** — a project-owned source of production calls from an agent platform. It holds what egma needs to find and import calls from the selected production targets.\\n+\\n+**monitoring import failure** — one production call that a monitoring source could not import after its allowed attempts. It remains visible so a user can inspect the failure and retry the import.\\n \\n ## What a simulation produces\\n*** End Patch"; text(await tools.apply_patch(patch)); 
